<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::arms_transfer_volume" target="global_financial_stress::short_term_market_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">+</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.55</data>
  <data key="d36">Larger, rapid arms transfers typically require accelerated government or corporate financing (advance payments, credit lines, or short-term issuance) in supplier and intermediary jurisdictions, tightening near-term funding markets and lifting short-term yields.</data>
  <data key="d37">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">global_financial_stress</data>
</edge>
<edge source="russia_ukraine_war::arms_transfer_volume" target="us_venezuelan_oil::geopolitical_influence" id="0">
  <data key="d27">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d33">-</data>
  <data key="d28">2</data>
  <data key="d35">0.6</data>
  <data key="d36">Larger Western arms transfers to Ukraine incentivize Russia to expand security- and energy-sector engagement with Caracas (military-technical support, credit-for-oil deals), strengthening Russian/partner leverage in Venezuela and thereby reducing the relative geopolitical influence of the US over Venezuelan oil policy.</data>
  <data key="d37">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d38">russia_ukraine_war</data>
  <data key="d39">us_venezuelan_oil</data>
</edge>
